<body>

<header>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Global Airbnb Market Comparison</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">Pricing, Supply & Guest Demand Across Major Cities</div>
        <div class="meta">
            <span>Audience: Hospitality Analysts & Market Strategists</span>
            <span>Data Source: Inside Airbnb (2026-03-29)</span>
            <span>50,000 Listings Analyzed</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</header>

<main class="container">

<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY / KEY TAKEAWAYS -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--text-light);margin-bottom:20px;">Synthesized findings for investors, hosts, and policymakers based on 50,000 Airbnb listings across seven cities.</p>

    <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Data Coverage Note:</strong> The dataset contains 50,000 listings across <strong>7 cities</strong> (London, Paris, Rome, Bangkok, Barcelona, Sydney, Amsterdam). <strong>New York, Tokyo, and Los Angeles are absent from this sample</strong> and are therefore excluded from all cross-market comparisons.
    </div>

    <ol class="takeaways">
        <li><strong>Bangkok commands the highest median nightly price ($1,400)</strong> — more than 6× Amsterdam ($222) and 10× London ($135). However, Bangkok's price distribution is heavily skewed by extreme outliers (max $102,858), suggesting possible local-currency artifacts or ultra-luxury inventory. The 99th-percentile-capped mean ($2,164) still confirms Bangkok as the premium market in this sample.</li>

        <li><strong>Professional host concentration is highest in Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%)</strong>, signaling heavily commercialized supply. Amsterdam (6.8%) and Paris (21.4%) remain comparatively individual-host markets. For regulators, Bangkok and Barcelona should be priority markets for enforcement of short-term rental caps.</li>

        <li><strong>Rome leads on guest-demand intensity</strong> with the highest average reviews per month (1.77), highest median reviews in the last 12 months (6.0), and the strongest review-recency profile (66.3% of listings reviewed within the past year). Rome's combination of high demand and moderate professional-host share (30.9%) suggests a healthy mix of commercial and organic supply.</li>

        <li><strong>Paris has the highest entire-home share (88.2%)</strong>, followed by Amsterdam (82.8%), indicating these markets are functionally apartment-rental platforms rather than home-sharing networks. Conversely, Barcelona's lower entire-home share (60.2%) reflects a more diverse room-type mix, partly due to higher private-room supply.</li>

        <li><strong>Booking-constraint patterns reveal regulatory footprints:</strong> Barcelona has the highest share of listings requiring ≥30 nights (40.1%), likely reflecting Catalonia's strict short-term rental laws. Sydney follows with 16.1% requiring ≥90 nights, pointing to New South Wales' 180-day caps and minimum-stay incentives. Paris (20.4% ≥30 nights) also shows constraint clustering consistent with its 120-day legal limit.</li>

        <li><strong>Amsterdam stands out as the most constrained market:</strong> lowest availability (92.5 days/year), lowest professional-host share (6.8%), and highest share of listings with zero availability (39.6%). This aligns with Amsterdam's progressive crackdown on short-term rentals in residential zones — the market is shrinking from the supply side.</li>

        <li><strong>London dominates by scale (33.3% of all listings)</strong> but shows middling performance on most metrics: median price $135, professional-host share 32.2%, and only 44.0% of listings with a recent review. Its large, fragmented supply suggests a maturing market with saturation risk in outer boroughs.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- MARKET OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>1. Global Market Overview</h2>
    <p>This section establishes the scale and composition of each market in the sample. London and Paris together account for over 61% of listings, while Amsterdam represents just 3.5%.</p>

    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c1', charts['chart1'])}</div>
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c4', charts['chart4'])}</div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th class="num">Listings</th>
                    <th class="num">Market Share</th>
                    <th class="num">Entire Home %</th>
                    <th class="num">Private Room %</th>
                    <th class="num">Hotel Room %</th>
                    <th class="num">Shared Room %</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

# Add table rows for market overview
room_counts = {}
for kpi in kpis:
    city = kpi['city']
    room_counts[city] = {
        'entire': kpi['entire_home_pct'],
        'private': 0, 'hotel': 0, 'shared': 0
    }

# We need actual room type percentages - compute from df
import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_csv('/app/benchmark/data.csv')
room_pct = pd.crosstab(df['city'], df['room_type'], normalize='index') * 100

for city in sorted(df['city'].unique()):
    row = room_pct.loc[city]
    total = len(df[df['city']==city])
    share = total / len(df) * 100
    html += f"""                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{city}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">{total:,}</td>
                    <td class="num">{share:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{row.get('Entire home/apt', 0):.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{row.get('Private room', 0):.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{row.get('Hotel room', 0):.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{row.get('Shared room', 0):.1f}%</td>
                </tr>
"""

html += """            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Insight:</strong> Paris and Amsterdam are the most "entire-home" dominated markets (>82%), while Barcelona has the most balanced mix with nearly 39% private rooms. This has direct implications for platform positioning — Barcelona retains more of the original "sharing economy" character.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- PRICING ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>2. Pricing Dynamics Across Cities</h2>
    <p>Pricing is the centerpiece of this analysis. We compare median and mean nightly prices by city and room type, highlighting dispersion and outliers.</p>

    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c2', charts['chart2'])}</div>
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c3', charts['chart3'])}</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Price Summary by City</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th class="num">Median Price</th>
                    <th class="num">Mean Price (Raw)</th>
                    <th class="num">Mean Price (Capped at 99th %ile)</th>
                    <th class="num">Std Dev</th>
                    <th class="num">IQR</th>
                    <th class="num">CV (%)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

# Pricing stats table
price_df = df.dropna(subset=['price'])
for city in sorted(price_df['city'].unique()):
    p = price_df[price_df['city'] == city]['price']
    p99 = p.quantile(0.99)
    capped = p.clip(upper=p99)
    html += f"""                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{city}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">${int(p.median())}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(p.mean())}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(capped.mean())}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(p.std())}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(p.quantile(0.75) - p.quantile(0.25))}</td>
                    <td class="num">{int(p.std()/p.mean()*100)}%</td>
                </tr>
"""

html += """            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Data Quality Alert:</strong> Bangkok's price data exhibits extreme right-tail skew (CV = 199%, max $102,858). While the median ($1,400) is robust, the raw mean ($2,337) is inflated by ~8% from outliers. The source brief notes that original Inside Airbnb data uses local currencies; some Bangkok listings may not be fully normalized to USD.
    </div>

    <h3>Price by Room Type (Median)</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th class="num">Entire Home/Apt</th>
                    <th class="num">Private Room</th>
                    <th class="num">Hotel Room</th>
                    <th class="num">Shared Room</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

room_med = price_df.groupby(['city', 'room_type'])['price'].median().unstack()
for city in sorted(room_med.index):
    row = room_med.loc[city]
    html += f"""                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{city}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">${int(row.get('Entire home/apt', 0)) if pd.notna(row.get('Entire home/apt')) else '—'}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(row.get('Private room', 0)) if pd.notna(row.get('Private room')) else '—'}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(row.get('Hotel room', 0)) if pd.notna(row.get('Hotel room')) else '—'}</td>
                    <td class="num">${int(row.get('Shared room', 0)) if pd.notna(row.get('Shared room')) else '—'}</td>
                </tr>
"""

html += """            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout success">
        <strong>Key Pricing Insight:</strong> Across all cities, entire homes command a premium over private rooms. The gap is widest in Bangkok (entire home median $1,500 vs. private room $1,087) and narrowest in Rome ($140 vs. $104). Hotel rooms are a small segment but priced competitively in London (median $246) and Barcelona ($192).
    </div>
</div>

<!-- SUPPLY STRUCTURE -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>3. Supply Structure & Host Concentration</h2>
    <p>Professional host concentration is a strategically critical metric. We define professional hosts as those with 5+ listings.</p>

    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c5', charts['chart5'])}</div>
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c6', charts['chart6'])}</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Host Concentration Metrics</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th class="num">Prof. Hosts (5+)</th>
                    <th class="num">Semi-Prof. (2–4)</th>
                    <th class="num">Individual (1)</th>
                    <th class="num">Mean Listings/Host</th>
                    <th class="num">Max Listings (Single Host)</th>
                    <th class="num">Super Hosts (20+)</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

for city in sorted(df['city'].unique()):
    subset = df[df['city'] == city]
    prof = (subset['calculated_host_listings_count'] >= 5).mean() * 100
    semi = ((subset['calculated_host_listings_count'] >= 2) & (subset['calculated_host_listings_count'] < 5)).mean() * 100
    ind = (subset['calculated_host_listings_count'] == 1).mean() * 100
    mean_list = subset['calculated_host_listings_count'].mean()
    max_list = int(subset['calculated_host_listings_count'].max())
    super_host = (subset['calculated_host_listings_count'] >= 20).mean() * 100
    html += f"""                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{city}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">{prof:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{semi:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{ind:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{mean_list:.1f}</td>
                    <td class="num">{max_list}</td>
                    <td class="num">{super_host:.1f}%</td>
                </tr>
"""

html += """            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout danger">
        <strong>Regulatory Signal:</strong> Barcelona has the highest super-host share (36.0% of listings from hosts with 20+ properties) and the highest mean listings per host (63.4). This level of concentration is a clear indicator of commercial operator dominance and should trigger regulatory scrutiny under Catalonia's 2024 short-term rental restrictions.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- GUEST DEMAND -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>4. Guest Demand & Review Activity</h2>
    <p>Reviews serve as a proxy for booking frequency and guest demand intensity. We analyze review volume, recency, and per-listing averages.</p>

    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c7', charts['chart7'])}</div>
        <div class="chart-box">{make_plotly_div('c8', charts['chart8'])}</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Demand Metrics by City</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th class="num">Avg Reviews/Month</th>
                    <th class="num">Avg Reviews (Total)</th>
                    <th class="num">Avg Reviews LTM</th>
                    <th class="num">Zero Reviews (%)</th>
                    <th class="num">Recent Review ≤12 Mo (%)</th>
                    <th class="num">Median Months Since Review</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
"""

scrape_date = pd.to_datetime('2026-03-29')
df['last_review_dt'] = pd.to_datetime(df['last_review'], errors='coerce')
df['months_since_review'] = (scrape_date - df['last_review_dt']).dt.days / 30.44

for city in sorted(df['city'].unique()):
    subset = df[df['city'] == city]
    avg_rpm = subset['reviews_per_month'].mean()
    avg_total = subset['number_of_reviews'].mean()
    avg_ltm = subset['number_of_reviews_ltm'].mean()
    zero_rev = (subset['number_of_reviews'] == 0).mean() * 100
    recent = (subset['months_since_review'] <= 12).sum() / len(subset) * 100
    med_months = subset.dropna(subset=['months_since_review'])['months_since_review'].median()
    html += f"""                <tr>
                    <td><strong>{city}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">{avg_rpm:.2f}</td>
                    <td class="num">{avg_total:.1f}</td>
                    <td class="num">{avg_ltm:.1f}</td>
                    <td class="num">{zero_rev:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{recent:.1f}%</td>
                    <td class="num">{med_months:.1f}</td>
                </tr>
"""

html += """            </tbody>
        </table>
