<!-- METHODOLOGY & DATA QUALITY -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>Methodology & Data Quality</h2>
    <p>This report is based on a single scrape of Inside Airbnb data dated <strong>2026-03-29</strong>. All statistics are computed directly from the provided <code>data.csv</code> file (50,000 rows). No external data or assumptions were used to fill gaps.</p>

    <h3>Known Limitations</h3>
    <ul style="margin-left:20px;color:var(--text-light);">
        <li><strong>Missing cities:</strong> New York, Tokyo, and Los Angeles are documented in the source brief but absent from <code>data.csv</code>. All cross-market rankings are therefore limited to the seven cities present.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing prices:</strong> 25,913 of 50,000 rows (51.8%) have null prices. Price statistics are computed only on non-null values and flagged accordingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Currency normalization:</strong> The source brief notes that prices are "assumed to be normalized to USD" but warns that original data uses local currencies. Bangkok's extreme outliers (max $102,858) suggest possible normalization gaps.</li>
        <li><strong>Review proxies:</strong> Reviews are an imperfect demand proxy. Not all guests leave reviews, and review frequency varies by culture and platform nudging.</li>
        <li><strong>neighbourhood_group and license columns:</strong> Largely empty across all cities and excluded from this analysis.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

</main>

